---
title: Who backed the company hiring you
date: 2026-08-01
summary: Job cards now carry the mark of the accelerator or fund that picked the company — Y Combinator, Techstars, a16z — and you can filter the feed by any of them.
type: changelog
tags:
  - job-seekers
  - filters
---

Whether a company was picked by Y Combinator says something a job description never
will. Until now you could only know it by recognising the company name.

Job cards now show the mark of the accelerator or fund that backed the employer, next
to the company name: **Y Combinator**, **Techstars**, and **a16z**. The same mark
appears on the job page, the company page, the company catalogue, and inside the
filter chips, so you can narrow the feed to one of them and see at a glance which
roles qualify.

a16z arrives as two separate filters rather than one, because they are not the same
fact. **a16z** is the fund's portfolio — companies it invested in, from seed-stage to
OpenAI. **a16z Speedrun** is the accelerator's cohorts: early companies that went
through its programme. A candidate choosing where to apply is looking at two very
different kinds of company, and collapsing them into one badge would have hidden that.

The other curated collections — Big Tech, Unicorns, Fortune 500, AI-Native and the
rest — stay as filters without a mark. They are themes we assembled ourselves, by
size or sector or geography; nobody selected their members, so there is no one whose
mark it would be.

One thing the badge does not say: it is a fact about the **company**, not about the
role in front of you. A fund backing an employer tells you nothing about a particular
posting's pay, its urgency, or whether anyone is reading the applications. It tells
you who looked at this company and decided to put money behind it — which is worth
knowing, and worth not over-reading.
